LESSON Introduction to Inferno
START intro

FRAME intro
TEXT
Welcome. This is a short branching lesson about Inferno, in the style
of PLATO's TUTOR language: type an answer and press Enter or Submit.

What is the name of the virtual machine that runs Inferno's compiled
bytecode?
END
JUDGE dis 1 disgood Right - it's called Dis.
JUDGE jvm 0 wrongjvm Close, but that's Java's virtual machine, not Inferno's.
JUDGE clr 0 wrongjvm Close, but that's .NET's virtual machine, not Inferno's.
DEFAULT 0 tryagain Not quite - think about what interprets .dis files.

FRAME tryagain
TEXT
Here's a hint: the compiled files this system runs end in ".dis".
What's the name of the machine that executes them?
END
JUDGE dis 1 disgood Right - it's called Dis.
DEFAULT 0 intro Let's go back to the start and try once more.

FRAME wrongjvm
TEXT
Not Inferno's own virtual machine - that one belongs to a different
system entirely. Inferno's is a two-letter name related to its own
file extension.
END
JUDGE dis 1 disgood Right - it's called Dis.
DEFAULT 0 intro Let's go back to the start and try once more.

FRAME disgood
TEXT
Correct! Inferno's virtual machine is called Dis.

Second question: what is the name of the language most Inferno
applications - including this lesson's own runtime - are written in?
END
JUDGE limbo 1 limbogood Right - Limbo.
JUDGE c 0 wrongc Inferno's own internals use C, but applications like this one don't.
DEFAULT 0 langtryagain Not quite - it's not one of the usual suspects like C or Java.

FRAME langtryagain
TEXT
Hint: the language's name is also a place - the edge of something,
neither fully one thing nor another.
END
JUDGE limbo 1 limbogood Right - Limbo.
DEFAULT 0 disgood Let's revisit that question.

FRAME wrongc
TEXT
C is what the Dis virtual machine itself and the emulator are written
in - but Inferno applications like this lesson are written one level
up, in a language made for this system specifically.
END
JUDGE limbo 1 limbogood Right - Limbo.
DEFAULT 0 disgood Let's revisit that question.

FRAME limbogood
TEXT
Correct! Applications like this one are written in Limbo.

That's the end of this short lesson. Well done - you've correctly
identified both the Dis virtual machine and the Limbo language.
END
DEFAULT 1 limbogood The lesson is complete - your score is shown above.
